#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# mathieu_s_prime

#% text
# `mathieu_s_prime(a, q, z)` gives the derivative in `z` of the odd Mathieu function `mathieu_s(a, q, z)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Numerical values: Taylor series of the equation re-expanded along the segment from 0, and the normalization from the characteristic exponent `nu` (`cos(pi nu) = w_I(pi)`, DLMF 28.2.16) and the Fourier coefficients of the Floquet solution (continued fractions, `sum c_k^2 = 1`). Real `a` and `q` use this normalization throughout; complex `a` or `q` use the principal branches and may differ from other conventions by the normalization.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
mathieu_s_prime(2, 1, 3.2)
#% output
#= -0.41459068965367946

#% cell
n(mathieu_s_prime(2, 1, 37/10), 20)
#% output
#= -0.21740542426975775339776

#% text
# A difference quotient of `mathieu_s`:

#% cell
(mathieu_s(2, 1, 3.2 + 1.0e-6) - mathieu_s(2, 1, 3.2 - 1.0e-6))/2.0e-6
#% output
#= -0.414590689723493

